# Mobile vs desktop UI

## Default

Use **one shared Liquid component tree**. Adapt with CSS media queries and light JS—do not fork full mobile and desktop implementations.

Liquid cannot know viewport width at render time. Breakpoint behavior belongs in CSS/JS.

## Breakpoints (theme standard)

- **Phone**: `max-width: 749px`
- **Tablet+**: `min-width: 750px` (`mediaQueryLarge` / `isMobileBreakpoint()` / `isDesktopBreakpoint()` in `assets/utilities.js`)
- **Desktop chrome**: `min-width: 990px` (header, page-wrapper scroll)

## Decision rule

| Situation | Approach |
|---|---|
| Same blocks, different layout/spacing/type | One component + CSS `@media` |
| Same blocks, different interaction | One component + `matchMedia` / existing breakpoint helpers |
| Truly different DOM that CSS `order`/grid cannot solve | Small local island only |
| Desktop-only merchant setting | Keep as a setting (e.g. `desktop_media_position`); do not invent a parallel mobile settings system |

## Local islands (rare)

When a fragment only exists on one breakpoint (e.g. mobile sticky ATC), use existing utilities—do not duplicate the whole page:

- `.mobile-only` / `.hidden--desktop` / `.desktop\:hidden` — visible on phone only
- `.desktop-only` / `.hidden--mobile` / `.mobile\:hidden` — visible on tablet+ only

Scope styles next to the component under `@media screen and (max-width: 749px)` (see `snippets/product-information-content.liquid`).

## Avoid

- Two full product/header/collection trees
- JS that rewrites large DOM on resize
- New parallel “mobile settings” systems when CSS can adapt the same markup
